A leading const binds to the first @ in a type suffix chain, so const T@[]@ resolves to an array of handles-to-const instead of a handle to a const array — and renders to the same display string as the correct const array<T@>@ (FIXED IN B119, filed for findability)


## FIXED IN B119, 2026-09-01 — filed for findability, not for action

Recorded as its own ticket because it is a **parser** defect that was found and
fixed inside a const-receiver ticket, and nobody hunting a parser bug would ever
look there.

## The defect

A leading `const` bound to the **first `@`** in a suffix chain:

| spelling | before | correct |
|---|---|---|
| `const C@[]@` | `Handle(Array(Handle(Const(C))))` | `Handle(Const(Array(Handle(C))))` |
| `const C@[]`  | `Array(Handle(Const(C)))`         | `Const(Array(Handle(C)))` |
| `const array<C@>@` | already correct | — |

**Two spellings of one type produced different `TypeRepr` trees with IDENTICAL
`display()` output.** That is why it survived: every string-level check agreed
while the structures diverged.

Fixed in `Parser::const_binding_at`, with the engine measurements behind each row
of the binding table.

## The family it belongs to

*"The check and the thing being checked agree because the check cannot see the
difference."*

Same shape as several entries in `docs/verification-hazards.md`. It is worth
stating as a standing caution for this codebase: **a test that compares types by
rendered string cannot detect a structural type bug.** Anywhere the display
string is the assertion, the assertion is blind to exactly this class of defect.

That caution generalises past types. It is also why B129's regen/drift design
needs care — a drift report that diffs RENDERED artifacts would miss precisely
this, and it is noted on that ticket.

## Guarantees now in place

- `the_const_rebind_never_changes_an_aliasing_verdict` constructs both trees and
  asserts B121's aliasing predicate agrees, so a future rebind that changed the
  OUTERMOST constructor — which would silently change B121's answer for every
  method returning that type — trips a test instead of shipping.
- The engine's actual semantics are pinned in
  `docs/probes/b119-const-receiver.tsv`: `arr.InsertLast(..)` REJECTED (the array
  is const), `@arr[0] = c` REJECTED (slot read-only), `arr[0].Mutate()` and
  `arr[0].x = 1` ACCEPTED (pointee mutable), and decisively `C@ e = arr[0];`
  ACCEPTED — an element binds to a NON-const handle, which `array<const C@>`
  would refuse.

## If you are here because something looks wrong

Check whether your test asserts on `display()`. If it does, it cannot see this
class of bug, and the fix is to assert on the constructed tree.